#N canvas 100 100 740 700 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 80 create;
#X msg 130 80 destroy;
#X obj 40 130 jet_head 0;
#X obj 40 170 t b b b;
#X obj 40 215 jet_rectangle 1 1;
#X obj 160 215 jet_separator;
#X obj 160 245 jet_translateXYZ -3.5 0 0;
#X obj 160 275 jet_color 1 0.3 0.3;
#X obj 160 305 jet_rectangle 1.2 1.2;
#X obj 380 215 jet_separator;
#X obj 380 245 jet_translateXYZ 3.5 0 0;
#X obj 380 275 jet_color 0.3 0.6 1;
#X obj 380 305 jet_triangle 1.5;
#X text 40 350 ^ centre: plain white \, fires LAST (t b b b goes right to left);
#X text 40 390 Result: a small white square in the middle \, a red one on the left and a blue triangle on the right. The middle shape is drawn after both branches \, yet it is not moved or coloured - because each branch is wrapped in a [jet_separator].;
#X text 40 470 [jet_separator] is Gem's [separator]: it remembers the chain's state (model transform \, colour \, depth on/off \, bound texture) \, forwards the bang to everything connected below it \, and then puts the state back. Without it \, a translate or colour in one branch would stay applied to whatever the chain draws next. Try it: connect the [t b b b] middle outlet directly to the left branch's [jet_translateXYZ] (bypassing the separator) and watch the order matter.;
#X text 40 580 Always use [t b b ...] to branch a chain \, never a plain fan-out: Pd does not define the order in which a single outlet's connections fire (it's the order you made the connections in \, which you can't see). The trigger fires right to left \, so you can say exactly what is drawn first.;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 4 1 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 4 2 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
